## Context

After unifying tool content formatting with `ComputerRuntime` (PR #13470), cloud sandbox now reuses local-system's `EditLocalFile` render component. This render displays a unified diff via `PatchDiff` component when `pluginState.diffText` is available.

## Problem

The cloud sandbox `editLocalFile` API does **not** return `diffText` in its response, causing the EditFile render to show nothing when expanded in sandbox mode.

## Expected Behavior

The sandbox `editLocalFile` should return `diffText` (unified diff format) alongside `replacements`, `linesAdded`, `linesDeleted` — matching the local-system implementation.

## Reference Implementation

**Local system** (`packages/local-file-shell/src/file/edit.ts` line 44-55):

```typescript
const patch = createPatch(filePath, oldContent, newContent, '', '');
const diffText = \`diff --git a\${filePath} b\${filePath}\n\${patch}\`;

let linesAdded = 0;
let linesDeleted = 0;
for (const line of patch.split('\n')) {
  if (line.startsWith('+') && !line.startsWith('+++')) linesAdded++;
  if (line.startsWith('-') && !line.startsWith('---')) linesDeleted++;
}

return { diffText, linesAdded, linesDeleted, replacements, success: true };
```

## Expected Response Shape

```typescript
interface EditLocalFileResult {
  diffText: string;      // unified diff — MISSING in sandbox
  linesAdded: number;    // already returned
  linesDeleted: number;  // already returned
  replacements: number;  // already returned
  success: boolean;      // already returned
}
```

## Consumer

`packages/builtin-tool-local-system/src/client/Render/EditLocalFile/index.tsx` — uses `pluginState.diffText` to render `<PatchDiff>`.

## Impact

Without `diffText`, the EditFile tool call in sandbox mode shows an empty render when expanded, while local-system mode correctly shows the diff.

## Problem

When using LobeChat with an OpenAI-compatible proxy (e.g., CLIProxyAPI, LiteLLM, One-API), GPT-5 series models fail with `"Unexpected end of JSON input"` because LobeChat forces these models to use the Responses API (`/v1/responses`) regardless of user settings.

The root cause is in `packages/model-runtime/src/core/openaiCompatibleFactory/index.ts` — the `shouldUseResponsesAPI()` method gives `responsesAPIModels` set (Priority 0) higher priority than the user's `enableResponseApi` toggle (Priority 1):

```typescript
// Priority 0: Check built-in responsesAPIModels FIRST (highest priority)
if (model && responsesAPIModels.has(model)) {
  return true; // ← No way for user to override this
}

// Priority 1: userApiMode is explicitly set to 'chatCompletion'
if (userApiMode === 'chatCompletion') {
  return false;
}
```

This means even when a user explicitly disables the "Responses API" toggle in provider settings, GPT-5 models still use `/v1/responses`.

## Why This Matters

Many OpenAI-compatible proxies (CLIProxyAPI, LiteLLM, One-API, vLLM) implement `/v1/chat/completions` perfectly but either:
- Don't implement `/v1/responses` at all
- Return responses in SSE format (`event: response.created`) that LobeChat's server-side streaming parser can't handle (it expects `data:` JSON chunks)

This makes all GPT-5 series models unusable through any OpenAI-compatible proxy, even though they work perfectly via `/v1/chat/completions`.

## Proposed Fix

Swap priorities so the user's explicit choice takes precedence:

```typescript
// Priority 0: userApiMode explicitly set (user's choice takes precedence)
if (userApiMode === 'chatCompletion') {
  return false;
}

// Priority 1: Check built-in responsesAPIModels
if (model && responsesAPIModels.has(model)) {
  return true;
}
```

This way:
- **Default behavior unchanged** — GPT-5 models still use Responses API when the toggle is enabled (default)
- **Users with proxies can opt out** — disabling the toggle forces all models to use Chat Completions API
- **No breaking changes** — only affects users who explicitly disable the toggle

## Environment

- LobeChat: latest (`lobehub/lobehub` Docker image)
- Deployment: server-side (Docker Compose with PostgreSQL)
- Proxy: CLIProxyAPI (OpenAI-compatible gateway for Anthropic/OpenAI/Google)
- Affected models: all GPT-5 series (`gpt-5.4`, `gpt-5.4-mini`, `gpt-5.2`, `gpt-5.1`, etc.)
- Working models: Claude series, Gemini series (not in `responsesAPIModels`)

## Reproduction Steps

1. Deploy LobeChat server-side with `OPENAI_PROXY_URL` pointing to an OpenAI-compatible proxy
2. Add GPT-5 models via `OPENAI_MODEL_LIST`
3. Disable "Responses API" toggle in OpenAI provider settings
4. Try chatting with any GPT-5 model → `"Unexpected end of JSON input"` error
5. Check proxy logs → LobeChat still sends to `/v1/responses` despite toggle being off

LobeHub runs agent teams that collaborate on real tasks. When agents use plugins and tools, the trust model is binary — either the plugin is installed or it isn't. There's no way to say "this agent can use the search plugin but not the email plugin" or "this agent can read emails but not send them" or "this team's total API spend is capped at $50/day."

For enterprise deployments with multiple teams sharing a LobeHub instance, the absence of scoped permissions means every agent in every team has access to every installed plugin. Plugin-level RBAC in application logic helps, but it runs in the same process as the agent — a sufficiently creative prompt injection can bypass application-level checks.

Delegation scoping enforces permissions at a layer the agent can't reach:

```typescript
import { createDelegation, governMCPToolCall } from 'agent-passport-system'

// Team lead delegates to support agent: CRM read + email send, nothing else
const supportDelegation = createDelegation({
  delegatedTo: supportAgentKey,
  delegatedBy: teamLeadKey,
  scope: ['plugin:crm:read', 'plugin:email:send'],
  spendLimit: 5000,  // $50 daily cap
  expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 8 * 3600_000),  // business hours
  maxDepth: 0
})

// Agent tries to use code execution plugin → blocked
const result = await governMCPToolCall(
  { name: 'code_interpreter', arguments: { code: 'os.system("rm -rf /")' } },
  async (args) => plugin.execute(args),
  { passport: agentPassport, delegation: supportDelegation, privateKey: agentKey }
)
// result.permitted === false — code_interpreter not in scope
// result.receipt — Ed25519-signed proof of the denied attempt
```

Each agent team gets its own delegation tree. Teams can't access each other's plugins because the delegation scopes don't overlap. Spend limits aggregate across the team. Every plugin call produces a signed receipt — the team lead can audit exactly what each agent did.

